# D&D AI — Environment Variables (example)
#
# Copy to .env (gitignored) and adjust. Per docs/PLAN.md §23.0 and ADR 0011,
# Production uses local PostgreSQL in the Ubuntu mini-PC Docker Compose
# topology. AWS/RDS variables below are transitional and optional. Runtime
# secrets come from host-mounted files or another external source; never commit
# them. See docs/LOCAL_DEPLOYMENT.md and ADR 0012.
# DATABASE_URL points at a LOCAL PostgreSQL 18 server by default — see
# docs/DEVELOPMENT.md §3 for install and setup. CI verifies the same work
# against the deployed AWS dev RDS instance; you rarely need to point at it
# yourself (docs/DEVELOPMENT.md §3.5).
#
# NEVER put a real credential in this file or in .env. Runtime secrets come from
# AWS Secrets Manager or IAM database authentication — see CLAUDE.md rule 10 and
# docs/DATABASE_CONVENTIONS.md §27.3. .env itself is gitignored, but treat any
# password you paste into DATABASE_URL as sensitive regardless.
#
# --- Application configuration source, by environment (src/dnd_ai/config.py) ---
#
# Production is selected ONLY by the real process/deployment environment —
# never by this file. DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT is checked directly against the real
# process environment before this file is even considered for loading; if
# that check already says "production", this file is never read at all. If it
# doesn't (this file's own DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT is unset or something else), this
# file DOES get loaded — but if IT then turns out to set DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT=
# production, that is treated as a configuration error and startup fails
# loudly, rather than quietly promoting the process into production sourced
# from a developer convenience file. Put another way: writing
# DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT=production in this file can only ever break startup, never
# select production — do not do it.
#
# local/test (the real environment's DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT is unset, "local", or
# "test" — the default): this file (resolved as <cwd>/.env) IS loaded, and
# every source below is accepted for database_url: DND_AI_DATABASE_URL, plain
# DATABASE_URL (this file's own convenience alias, already used by
# Alembic/pytest/CI), or — if neither is set — a hardcoded local-dev default.
#
# production (the real environment's DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT=production): this
# file is never loaded, by design — production reads only real process
# environment variables and mounted secret files, never a developer
# convenience file that might be sitting around. Only DND_AI_DATABASE_URL
# satisfies startup, supplied either as a real environment variable OR as a
# mounted secret file named exactly `dnd_ai_database_url` under
# DND_AI_SECRETS_DIR (default /run/secrets — so by default:
# /run/secrets/dnd_ai_database_url). If both are present, the environment
# variable wins (pydantic-settings' normal source precedence — deliberately
# inherited, not overridden, here). Plain DATABASE_URL and the local-dev
# default are both refused; Settings() raises immediately rather than
# silently starting against the wrong database. None of this is inferred by
# inspecting the URL's text (e.g. whether it mentions "localhost") — only by
# which source actually supplied it.
#
# Application settings are namespaced under DND_AI_ so they never collide
# with — or silently swallow a typo in — the AWS/Terraform variables also
# kept in this shared file. That namespace is shared with a few *other*
# subsystems' variables, though — DND_AI_TEST_DATABASE_URL and
# DND_AI_CI_DB_NAME (the test harness/CI scripts' own ephemeral-database
# variables — see tests/conftest.py, scripts/ci_ephemeral_database.py) and
# DND_AI_SEEDS_DIR (src/dnd_ai/persistence/seeds.py) are real, valid, and
# never set here (they're harness/CI-managed) — but a typo of any
# DND_AI_*-prefixed name, whether one of Settings' own fields or one of
# those three, still fails Settings() outright rather than being ignored.

# =============================================================================
# Application
# =============================================================================

DND_AI_ENVIRONMENT=local
DND_AI_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Where to read one-file-per-setting host-mounted secrets from (the
# conventional Docker/Compose secrets mount) — optional locally, since a
# nonexistent directory is simply skipped. Always a real environment
# variable, never something this file itself supplies in production (see
# above) — it names *where other secrets live*.
# DND_AI_SECRETS_DIR=/run/secrets

# =============================================================================
# Database — local PostgreSQL 18 (default)
# =============================================================================
# The major version MUST match what RDS runs (docs/DATABASE_CONVENTIONS.md
# §2.1). A local server on a different major version produces green local runs
# that fail CI — that is a setup defect, not a preference.
#
# Setup: install PostgreSQL 18, then `createdb -U postgres dnd_ai`. The six
# project roles are created by the 001_bootstrap migration, not by hand.
#
# Note there is no sslmode here: a stock local server has no TLS configured,
# whereas dev's parameter group sets rds.force_ssl=1 and rejects a plain
# connection outright. That asymmetry is the most common surprise when you do
# connect to dev.

# Read by Alembic, pytest, and CI directly, and by the application as a
# local/test-only compatibility alias (see above) — production never reads
# this file at all, so this value can never reach a production process by
# accident; production sets DND_AI_DATABASE_URL as a real environment
# variable or mounted secret instead.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/dnd_ai

# Separate database for manual local runs — for poking around with psql, not
# read by Settings or by pytest, which provisions its own ephemeral database
# per session regardless (as DND_AI_TEST_DATABASE_URL, a *different*,
# harness-managed variable pytest sets for itself — see tests/conftest.py —
# not something you set here).
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/dnd_ai_test

# =============================================================================
# Database — AWS dev (occasional)
# =============================================================================
# Only needed to reproduce a CI-only failure or inspect dev directly. Open
# network access first with `scripts/aws-db-allow-my-ip.sh open`, and close it
# when you're done — it is not automatic outside CI.
#
# Build this from Terraform outputs plus the master secret's password:
#   terraform -chdir=terraform/environments/dev output database_endpoint
#   aws secretsmanager get-secret-value \
#     --secret-id $(terraform -chdir=terraform/environments/dev output -raw database_secret_name) \
#     --query SecretString --output text
#
# `sslmode=require` is not optional against dev — see the note above.
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://dnd_admin:<password>@<endpoint>:5432/dnd_ai?sslmode=require

# =============================================================================
# AWS
# =============================================================================
# Not required for schema or application work (docs/CONTRIBUTING.md §2).

AWS_REGION=us-east-1
AWS_PROFILE=dnd-ai-dev

# =============================================================================
# Secrets Manager references
# =============================================================================
# Names only — the application resolves values at runtime. Confirm the actual
# names with: terraform -chdir=terraform/environments/dev output

# SECRET_OPENAI=dnd-ai/dev/openai/api-key
# SECRET_DISCORD=dnd-ai/dev/discord/bot-token

# =============================================================================
# Feature flags
# =============================================================================

DND_AI_FEATURE_AI_NPC_DIALOGUE=false
DND_AI_FEATURE_DISCORD_INTEGRATION=false
DND_AI_FEATURE_FOUNDRY_INTEGRATION=false
